{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 1, :comments ["<b>And this is the blessing.</b> One needs to know why it says ״וְזֹאת״ with the addition of a vav at the beginning of the matter. Further, one needs to know for what purpose the entire verse is said: if to inform us of the one who blesses and the ones who are blessed — are these not recognizable without this verse, if it had begun from the verse ״וַיֹּאמֶר״ etc.? And if to inform us that this was before his death, close to his passing — these matters are understood from what precedes it and what follows it, that the things were close to his death. And moreover, what need is there in informing us of the time?" "<b>And it appears</b> that the verse intends to praise the man of Elohim, how great were his deeds. For in the preceding parashah, close to the statement ״וְזֹאת הַבְּרָכָה״ before it, the verse stated that the decree was finalized upon Moses to die, in His saying (Deuteronomy 32:49): ״עֲלֵה אֶל הַר הָעֲבָרִים״ etc. ״וּמֻת״ etc. ״עַל אֲשֶׁר״ etc. — and if so, a speaker might say that this Moses had a grievance in his heart concerning his death, which had a cause together with the children of Israel, since he ran after them like a horse (Midrash Tanchuma) — they caused him to die outside the land and prevented him from entering the land that his soul longed for and craved. And human nature pushes away one who surrounds evil, and even though it is known that the righteous man is capable of bearing it after the fact, this helps so that he not hate them, but closeness of heart is nevertheless withheld. Therefore the word of HaShem in His Torah came to testify concerning the righteous man and said that not only did he not hate them and did not distance them from his heart, he still had ״this blessing״ etc. for them. And therefore it says ״וְזֹאת״ with the addition of a vav to alert to the preceding point that follows from the adjacent statement — and understand." "And it finished by saying ״before his death,״ meaning close to his passing, to testify further to the surpassing degree of his righteousness — that even at the time of provocation, which is the moment of departure, when the matter would compel hatred, nevertheless he blessed them. And the verse testified concerning the blessing that it was superior and wholehearted with all his heart, in that it wrote a vav at the beginning of the word, to alert us to how great was its magnitude." "<b>Further,</b> it intends by saying ״וְזֹאת״ etc., that since the blessings of others preceded — Abraham blessed Isaac, and Isaac blessed Jacob, and Jacob blessed his sons, and all of them gathered to the head of Israel — therefore when Moses came to bless Israel he was precise in his words, for his blessing is an addition to the earlier ones, in the manner that they of blessed memory explained concerning a blessing (Sifrei) on the verse (Deuteronomy 1:11): ״HaShem Elohei of your fathers add״ etc. ״and bless you״ etc. — that the intention is: just as I bless you, so may HaShem add and bless you etc. — see how he insists on being precise that he is adding blessing. And likewise he testified here that his blessing is an addition to the blessings of the earlier ones, and this is what he alluded to in the statement ״וְזֹאת.״" "And its saying ״the blessing״ with the definite article, the Torah testifies that it is the superior and most praiseworthy of all of them, and the verse comes to say the things through which it is exalted above every blessing." "<b>[The letter alef:]</b> ״whom Moses blessed״ — and his greatness is well known, as the eternal Elohim wrote when He rebuked Aaron and Miriam (Numbers 12:7), as I explained there." "<b>[The letter bet:]</b> ״the man of Elohim״ — go out and learn how greatly the Rabbis of blessed memory (Midrash Tanchuma 2) exerted themselves to expound this phrase, going so far as to say that it is in the manner of the verse (Ruth 1:3) ״Naomi's man,״ and the intention is that Moses would decree and HaShem would fulfill, and understand this. And see what I expounded with Heaven's help in the vicinity:" "<b>[The letter gimel:]</b> ״et the children of Israel״ — for they are a prepared vessel to receive blessings such as had not existed until now. And go out and learn that the Shekhinah did not rest upon them collectively except upon the children of Israel, because the forces of holiness gathered together — which number sixty myriads. And the verse says ״et the children of Israel״ and did not suffice to say merely ״children״ — perhaps it intends to include with them that which is hidden from the eyes of all living, which is the Shekhinah, which is with Israel even in distress, as it is written (Psalms 91:15) ״I am with him in distress.״ And knowledge of the opposite is equivalent: Moses intended that he be the attendant to bless her together with them." "<b>[The letter dalet:]</b> ״before his death״ — for although the measure of Moses' greatness is immense, his spirit and power increase at the time when the moment arrives for him to be gathered to his people. And on account of these four reasons, HaShem magnified and singled it out above all of them by saying ״And this is the blessing.״" "<b>Further,</b> by saying ״and this״ he alludes, in the manner of their words of blessed memory (Yalkut Shimoni, Sifrei here), that Moses did not merit being called ״the man of Elohim״ until he blessed Israel. And this is what he signaled by saying ״and this״ — meaning, besides all that he accomplished and did in uprightness, when he added this blessing with which Moses blessed the children of Israel, by this he was called ״the man of Elohim.״" "<b>A man of Elohim.</b> The verse may be explained along the lines of what they said, may their memory be for blessing (Arakhin 17a), and this is their language: if the Holy One, blessed be He, comes to sit in judgment with Abraham, Isaac, and Jacob, they are unable to stand before Him — end quote. From this you have learned that what the righteous accomplish through their deeds and their self-strengthening in this world is effective only by virtue of the power of mercy, but not in judgment; as David said (Psalms 143:2): ״וְאַל תָּבוֹא בְמִשְׁפָּט אֶת עַבְדֶּךָ כִּי לֹא יִצְדַּק לְפָנֶיךָ כָּל חָי״ — meaning, any righteous person who may be called חי. Here, the verse informs us that this righteous one strengthened himself and effected good even in the aspect of the dimension of judgment alluded to in the name ״Elohim,״ and that is its saying ״a man of Elohim״ — and if HaShem were to come to stand with him in judgment, he could withstand, for he excelled wondrously in doing good and what is upright." "<b>Further,</b> this statement intends to testify about him that all his strengthening in the known good traits was solely on account of his fear of his Elohim — not that his nature assisted him toward this, as they said, may their memory be for blessing (Yoma 22b): Saul with one [offense] and it did not avail him, and David etc. — for Saul was by nature even-tempered and was not hot and impulsive to err, and therefore over one error the kingship departed from him according to his nature. The ones on high estimate that if David's nature, being ruddy [hot-blooded], had been like Saul's, he would have sinned tenfold. And as for the man Moses, there is room to think that he was humble by nature and was forbearing in all things by nature; therefore the Torah testified about him that all his self-strengthening and might, alluded to in the word ״אִישׁ,״ was on account of the Elohim whom he feared — and that is its saying ״a man of Elohim.״" "<b>Further,</b> it alludes in this direction through ״a man of Elohim״ — that none arose and none will arise like him. And the verse intends, by juxtaposing ״אֶת בְּנֵי יִשְׂרָאֵל״ to the statement ״a man of Elohim,״ to say that he attained this rank together with the children of Israel — meaning, through the exertion he devoted to them and the burden he bore from them, and the branching-out [of spiritual benefit] that came through his good conduct with them, he attained this rank of being called a man of Elohim."], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 33, :notes {7 "The phrase 'knowledge of the opposite is equivalent' (וידיעת ההפכים שוה) likely refers to the inverse logical point — namely that just as Israel needs the Shekhinah, so the Shekhinah is completed through Israel.", 11 "The bracketed word סַבְלָן (forbearing) appears in the source text; it is included in the translation."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:22:32.507169354Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}